[{"role": "system", "content": "You maintain `skill.md`, a short, general reference of design lessons for an automated process that's evolving a population of solitaire game variants (written in a custom format called SGDL) generation by generation.\n\nEach generation, many independently-running variants are each given exactly one focused revision based on their own simulated playtest results, by a separate process that has no visibility into what any of the other variants are doing. Whoever makes each revision also writes a short \"insight\": a general, transferable lesson suggested by that one specific change and its outcome, not a narration of what they did. You'll be given the current skill.md (or told it doesn't exist yet, if this is the first generation) plus the full batch of insights produced this generation, and your job is to produce the next version of skill.md.\n\nTreat skill.md as a living document you rewrite each time, not a log you append to:\n\n- Integrate new insights into the existing structure rather than tacking them on. If several insights from this generation (or an insight and an existing point) are really saying the same thing in different words, merge them into one clearer, possibly stronger statement -- don't keep near-duplicates as separate bullets.\n- If this generation's evidence contradicts or weakens an existing point, revise or remove it rather than letting both versions stand.\n- Look for what's well-supported. If most of this generation's relevant insights agree, that's worth keeping or strengthening; if only one or two insights suggest something that doesn't fit with everything else, it may just be noise from one particular variant rather than a real general lesson, and it's fine to leave out.\n- Write every point as a general principle about solitaire design, not as a fact about one specific file's exact numbers (\"foundation piles that no move rule ever targets sit permanently empty regardless of other changes\" is useful; \"Trizug's draw pile of 24 cards was too small\" is not, since nobody will recognize \"Trizug\" later).\n- Organize the document under a small number of clear headers (e.g. draw pile design, difficulty and winnability, avoiding unused elements, win conditions), and keep the whole thing short enough that it's actually useful as a quick reference -- a sprawling document nobody can read in full defeats the purpose. A specific cap on total bullet points is given to you below; treat it as a hard limit, not a target to approach.\n- It's completely fine for this to be a light touch-up most generations and a more substantial rewrite occasionally, depending on what the evidence actually supports.\n\nEnd your response with the complete new skill.md -- the whole document, not a diff -- in a single fenced code block tagged `skill`, as the last thing in your response. You can reason about what to keep, merge, or change beforehand.\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Current skill.md:\n\n```skill\n# skill.md\n\n## Establish a valid, playable core\n- Treat compile failures, bad deck accounting, invalid references, impossible predicates, rule-slot conflicts, and vacuous win clauses as first-order design failures. Restore a runnable game before tuning difficulty.\n- Prefer the smallest count-preserving, role-preserving repair. A runnable near-neighbor reveals more than a speculative redesign layered on top of an invalid file.\n- Re-check setup, move rules, sink rules, and win clauses together after every structural fix. Nonstandard suit/color makeup, duplicated suits, multiple deck copies, and engine-specific rule contexts can make familiar-looking rules unreachable, illegal, or impossible.\n- Keep move definitions compact and non-overlapping. If the engine effectively allows one rule per source/destination pair, merge exceptions into one condition tree instead of splitting them into competing moves.\n- Treat stock/deal piles according to their actual engine role. A deal-only stock is setup/feeding infrastructure unless the rules explicitly support direct play from it.\n\n## Align sinks, deck structure, and victory\n- If the win condition requires active piles to empty, the design needs a reachable, irreversible sink that can absorb the entire deck under ordinary play. Rearrangement alone is not a completion plan.\n- Verify both sink capacity and sink legality against the real deck structure. In reduced-suit, duplicated-suit, or multi-copy decks, foundation count, start rules, matching rules, and target lengths must scale to the actual completion units present.\n- Align victory with where irreversible progress actually accumulates. A one-way receiving area with no reliable release path should usually count as progress toward winning, not be something the player must later empty.\n- Match the sink to the pattern the game naturally assembles. Sinks that accept frequent, ordinary incremental progress create real completion; sinks that only reward rare perfect chunks mostly create churn.\n- In repeated-suit, reduced-suit, or broad-compatibility tableau games, standard exact-suit ace-up foundations are often the wrong completion model. Broader sink compatibility or run-based extraction can fit the deck\u2019s real completion units better than adding more tableau freedom.\n- Keep one-way destinations purposeful. A pile that only receives cards should either be the true sink or have a strong release path; otherwise it becomes dead storage, false progress, and search noise.\n- Avoid over-provisioned or decorative sinks. Extra sink piles beyond the deck\u2019s real completion needs, or receivers that are not tied to victory, blur pile roles and can make the game weaker or even impossible.\n\n## Shape mobility to create progress, not churn\n- Distinguish deadness from churn. Very short exhausted losses usually indicate missing completion paths or poor opening contact; long high-usage losses and search-cap failures usually indicate reversible motion disconnected from victory.\n- When mobility is high but wins are absent, fix sink mismatch before adding more freedom. Broadening sink compatibility, repurposing an existing one-way area into the main sink, or changing the rewarded completion pattern is usually higher leverage than loosening tableau rules.\n- Favor movement rules that build in the same direction the sink rewards. Tableau, stack, deal, and extraction rules should help expose and assemble sink-ready structures instead of demanding one pattern in play and another at completion.\n- If some wins already exist but most losses hit search limits, tighten the broadest reversible routes before adding mobility. Empty-pile fills, helper-pile parking, redeals, and oversized stack transfers are common churn sources.\n- Prefer constrained workspace over generic storage. Rank-gated empties, single-card access, limited redeals, or stack rules that mirror ordinary building preserve planning value better than unrestricted parking.\n- If a game is already winning too consistently, the safest difficulty lever is often to narrow the broadest reversible move rather than to weaken the sink or add new structure.\n\n## Tune openings and pacing with small setup changes\n- When losses exhaust in 0\u20134 moves across many shuffles, first check for a real completion path. If the sink is valid, then treat the problem as opening contact.\n- If some deals already win but many others die immediately, prefer small count-preserving exposure or layout changes over broad rules loosening. More face-up information, more even tableau depths, or one more live pile often improves shuffle fairness without flattening the game.\n- When fully exposed layouts still stall immediately, the bottleneck may be specific contact rules or stock access rather than hidden information. Relax the gating rule that blocks early participation before adding broad new mobility.\n- Use count-preserving setup changes to tune contact and pacing. Redistributing the same cards across more or shallower active piles can improve exposure, workspace, congestion, and deal rhythm at once.\n```\n\nThis generation produced 100 insight(s) from independently revised variants:\n\n1. When a solitaire produces lots of legal rearrangement but no wins, look first for sink mismatch rather than mobility shortage: if tableau building and the completion area reward different patterns, the game often devolves into churn. A good sink should accept structures the player can naturally assemble during ordinary play and should count as irreversible progress toward victory.\n2. When a solitaire already wins often but remaining losses hit search limits rather than exhausting quickly, look first for over-broad reversible structure or decorative piles. Trimming an unnecessary sink or pile can reduce search noise and clarify progress more effectively than adding more movement freedom.\n3. When a DEAL stock feeds multiple piles at once, its size should align with the number of receiving piles; otherwise the last partial deal can make the stock effectively unusable or undefined.  \nFor one-way sink piles, a strong win check is usually to test that they have reached their intended final sizes, rather than trying to reference the stock directly in victory.\n4. When a game shows long, high-usage losses with almost no proven wins, the problem is often not lack of mobility but lack of a sink that matches the structures play naturally creates. A one-way receiving area should reward the game\u2019s common local pattern\u2014like pairing cards to a target sum\u2014rather than a rarer or unrelated sequence.\n5. When a reduced-suit deck removes one color entirely, any `alternate_color` rule becomes vacuous and can silently kill the whole game. In custom solitaire variants, always re-check tableau compatibility rules against the actual suit/color composition before tuning difficulty or adding new structure.\n6. When a variant exhausts in just a few moves on every shuffle, first check whether the game has a sink that can realistically absorb the whole deck. A one-way receiving pile should usually accept the same pattern the tableau naturally creates; otherwise it becomes decorative storage instead of a completion path.\n7. When a solitaire already has real wins with full card and pile usage, but many losses exhaust in 0\u20134 moves, the problem is often opening contact rather than core rules. A small, count-preserving redistribution into more even tableau piles can improve fairness and early decision density without making the game trivial.\n8. When a pile type is one-way receiving storage, victory should usually treat it as completed progress rather than demand that it later be emptied. Long, high-usage losses with no wins often indicate not a lack of mobility, but a sink/win mismatch.\n9. When play generates lots of reversible motion but no wins, first ask whether the irreversible sink matches the deck\u2019s real completion units. A one-way receiving area should usually align directly with victory; otherwise it becomes search noise and false progress rather than a path to completion.\n10. When fixing an invalid solitaire file, repair compile legality and completion logic before tuning difficulty. A one-way receiving pile should usually be treated as the game\u2019s actual sink in the win condition, especially when it already has the right count and structure to absorb the full deck.\n11. When a solitaire produces long search-cap losses with moderate-to-high card usage, the usual problem is not lack of mobility but a sink that rewards structures the normal play rules rarely assemble. A stronger design often comes from making completion incremental and aligned with ordinary building patterns, then letting victory count that irreversible progress directly.\n12. When a rules engine treats the draw pile as a special action source rather than a normal pile type, victory should be phrased in terms of the playable pile types and the irreversible sinks, not by referencing `DRAW` directly. More generally, if one-way foundations are the game\u2019s true progress sink, the win condition should measure completion there rather than asking the player to undo that progress.\n13. If a solitaire requires the main play area to empty, make sure there is a one-way destination that both accepts ordinary progress and counts directly toward victory. A receiver that absorbs cards but is excluded from the win condition turns apparent progress into a proof of impossibility.\n14. When an engine allows only one rule per source/destination pair, overlapping source sets can break compilation even if the move ideas are sensible. Preserve the design by merging overlapping moves into a single condition tree or a broader source set before changing gameplay structure.\n15. When a solitaire shows high card usage but zero wins at the search cap, suspect a sink/victory mismatch before adding more tableau freedom. In repeated single-suit decks, exact-suit foundations often add no useful restriction; what matters more is whether the sink accepts the pattern the game can naturally build and whether victory acknowledges cards parked there.\n16. When a solitaire already produces long, high-usage search failures, the main problem is often not lack of movement but that the irreversible sink is too narrow for the structures ordinary play can assemble. Broadening sink compatibility in the same direction cards naturally get organized is usually more effective than adding yet more tableau freedom.\n17. When a game has a one-way receiving area that can absorb the full deck, victory should usually be tied to filling that sink rather than emptying the source area. Otherwise the design can become mathematically impossible even if ordinary moves are legal.\n18. When some deals already win cleanly but many others are exhausted almost immediately, the completion model may be fine and the real issue is opening contact. In reduced-suit or same-color decks, exact-suit tableau building can make starts unnaturally brittle; relaxing it to a broader compatibility that still preserves rank structure often improves fairness without trivializing the game.\n19. When a game already has some wins but many shuffles are proven impossible, the highest-leverage fix is often the sink, not the tableau. In duplicated or reduced-suit decks, foundations that demand exact suit progression can invalidate otherwise workable layouts; broadening sink compatibility to match the deck\u2019s real completion units often improves solvability more cleanly than adding more reversible movement.\n20. When a solitaire shows long, high-usage losses with little exhaustion, the problem is often not lack of moves but lack of irreversible progress. Replacing a rare \u201cperfect chunk only\u201d sink with an incremental sink that accepts the same ordering the tableau already builds can turn churn into meaningful advancement without rewriting the whole game.\n21. When the engine forbids certain pile references in victory checks, repair the win condition by pointing it at the game\u2019s true irreversible progress area rather than trying to force the stock itself into the goal. In solitaire, a receiving pile with no release path usually should count as completed progress.\n22. When a solitaire already wins sometimes but most losses are long, non-exhaustive searches, the problem is often reversible state churn rather than lack of raw mobility. A good next step is to make auxiliary piles more specialized\u2014especially by removing stack transfers through them\u2014so they stay useful as workspace without becoming generic reshuffling hubs.\n23. When a solitaire shows high card/pile usage but zero wins, the problem is often not mobility but that the irreversible sink rewards a pattern different from the one ordinary play naturally builds. Converting a side area from incremental storage into a completion sink for the tableau\u2019s native structure can reduce churn without simply making the game looser.\n24. When a solitaire already has some full-deck wins but many shuffles die immediately, prefer a count-preserving layout redistribution before broadening move rules. More, shallower active piles often improves opening contact and decision density without turning the game into unrestricted churn.\n25. If victory requires certain piles to empty, make sure the remaining receiving areas have enough total capacity and legal access to absorb the whole deck. A rearrangement-only system with less sink capacity than deck size is not \u201chard\u201d \u2014 it is simply unwinnable.\n26. When a solitaire already has a valid completion path and some real wins, but many losses hit the search cap instead of exhausting, look first for overly broad reversible parking moves. Restricting who can fill empty tableau spaces often improves decision quality more than adding new mobility.\n27. When a solitaire already has occasional full wins but most losses hit search limits with high card/pile usage, the problem is often reversible churn rather than lack of mobility. In that situation, tightening the broadest repetition source\u2014especially unlimited stock redeals\u2014can improve practical solvability and decision quality more than adding new moves.\n28. When a solitaire has many fully exhausted losses, check whether every card can actually reach an irreversible sink under ordinary play. If victory requires active areas to empty, a broad, reachable sink is often a higher-leverage fix than adding more tableau mobility.\n29. When a game already has a one-way receiving area that can absorb the whole deck, make victory track that irreversible progress directly. Requiring players to empty other active piles afterward often turns successful completion into unnecessary churn and search noise rather than meaningful challenge.\n30. When a solitaire already wins often but occasional losses are proven very early, prefer small count-preserving layout redistributions before changing core move rules. Slightly flattening tableau depths can improve opening contact and rescue dead shuffles without erasing the game\u2019s existing decision structure.\n31. When a solitaire uses a reduced suit set or repeated same-color suits, standard exact-suit foundations can become an artificial bottleneck even if tableau play is lively. A good sink should match the deck\u2019s natural equivalence classes closely enough that ordinary play can convert mobility into irreversible progress.\n32. When a solitaire has plenty of rearrangement but no wins, first check whether its one-way receiving piles reward the same structures the tableau naturally builds. A sink that accepts ordinary incremental progress is usually more effective than a foundation model borrowed from a different deck structure.\n33. When a stock is a deal-only feeder rather than a normal playable pile, don't make victory depend on naming or emptying it unless the engine explicitly allows that in win conditions. In stock-fed designs, first restore a valid sink-and-victory loop with the active piles before tuning difficulty.\n34. When a tableau game already has a real completion path and many wins, but some losses are fully exhausted from the start, the next lever is often opening contact rather than rule loosening. A small, count-preserving redistribution into more/shallow active piles can reduce dead shuffles while preserving the game\u2019s core decisions.\n35. When a variant already wins on every shuffle, first tighten the most generous reversible move instead of weakening the sink. Restricting how empty spaces can be used often increases planning and decision value without making the core completion path incoherent.\n36. When most deals exhaust immediately, first verify that the game has a reachable irreversible path to victory at all. A one-way receiver that does not count toward completion is usually dead storage; repurposing it into the actual sink is often more valuable than adding more tableau freedom.\n37. When a solitaire shows high card/pile usage but only search-cap losses, the problem is often not insufficient mobility but a sink that doesn't match the deck\u2019s natural completion units. Converting a one-way holding area into a true, incremental victory sink is often more effective than loosening tableau rules further.\n38. When a variant fails basic deck accounting, prefer a minimal count-preserving repair that preserves the intended pile roles and move system. Getting back to a runnable near-neighbor is usually more informative than redesigning rules before the game can even be simulated.\n39. When a solitaire shows high usage and long search-cap losses across all runs, the problem is often not lack of mobility but a sink that doesn't match the deck's actual duplication structure. In repeated-suit or reduced-suit decks, broadening sink compatibility to the deck\u2019s natural completion units can unlock real progress without making tableau play much looser.\n40. When every deal is exhaustively unwinnable and usage stays at zero, look first for a missing completion route or an unreachable stock, not for subtle balance issues. A one-way receiving area should usually accept frequent incremental progress and count toward victory; requiring rare perfect chunks while also demanding the tableau empty is a common way to make a solitaire structurally impossible.\n41. When a solitaire shows high card usage but zero wins with search-cap losses, the likely problem is not mobility but that the game\u2019s irreversible progress is aimed at the wrong place. If auxiliary receiving piles already exist, turning them into the true completion sink can be a cleaner fix than adding yet more tableau freedom.\n42. When a solitaire shows high card/pile usage but no wins and frequent search-cap losses, suspect reversible churn rather than lack of mobility. In those cases, improving the completion sink so it matches a clear, irreversible deck-partitioning pattern is often more effective than adding yet more tableau freedom.\n43. When the engine effectively permits only one move rule per source/destination pair, merge special cases into a single condition tree instead of splitting them into parallel rules. Also, if a pile acts as the main one-way receiving area, victory should usually recognize cards accumulating there rather than requiring unrelated helper piles to empty.\n44. When a game uses a repeated single-suit deck, the win condition should usually scale to the deck\u2019s real completion units. If one completed run can end the game while most cards remain irrelevant, the sink is under-aligned with the deck and produces shallow, low-usage wins.\n45. When a game\u2019s tableau builds one pattern but its only sink rewards a different, much rarer pattern, play tends to devolve into high-usage churn with no completion. A strong repair is to make irreversible progress accumulate through frequent, incremental sink moves that match what the rest of the rules naturally expose and assemble.\n46. When a solitaire already has a valid sink and many wins, but a minority of deals are proven unwinnable after very few moves, the next lever is often opening contact rather than rule looseness. A small, count-preserving redistribution into more shallow piles can improve fairness and decision density without changing the game\u2019s core logic.\n47. When a solitaire shows long, non-exhaustive losses with moderate card usage, suspect churn caused by misaligned progress systems before adding more freedom. In particular, make sure the tableau-building pattern, movable-stack pattern, and irreversible sink all reward the same structure.\n48. When a one-way receiving pile is the only place irreversible progress can accumulate, victory should usually be defined around filling it rather than requiring some other active area to empty under incompatible rules. In reduced-suit or repeated-copy decks, broadening sink compatibility to match the deck\u2019s natural grouping can fix impossible games more effectively than adding tableau mobility.\n49. When a solitaire shows high card/pile usage but zero wins and repeated search-cap losses, the problem is often not lack of mobility but lack of a sink that matches the structures the game naturally builds. Converting a one-way receiver from a rare, exact-pattern foundation into a sink for common in-play patterns can reduce churn far more effectively than adding yet more tableau freedom.\n50. When a solitaire shows long, high-usage, search-cap losses, the problem is often not insufficient mobility but a sink that doesn't match the structures the game naturally forms. In repeated-suit decks, rewarding complete same-suit runs as irreversible extraction can convert churn into real progress better than standard ace-up foundations.\n51. When a reduced-suit deck removes color contrast entirely, \"same suit only\" tableau and sink rules can eliminate almost all opening contact. In such layouts, broadening compatibility to match the deck\u2019s actual suit/color structure is often a more effective repair than adding more workspace or draw mechanics.\n52. When a solitaire already has some wins but most losses hit search limits with high card usage, look first for reversible move loops rather than lack of mobility. Tightening the broadest tableau exception is often a better next step than adding new freedom, especially if the sink already supports completion.\n53. When a game shows both immediate exhausted losses and long search-cap losses, check whether irreversible progress is actually aligned with victory before adding more mobility. A one-way receiving pile that the win condition ignores often creates false progress and search churn; making it the explicit sink is usually higher leverage than loosening tableau moves.\n54. When a solitaire variant already wins often but its losses hit search limits with high pile usage, the problem is often reversible empty-space parking rather than lack of mobility. Tightening the broadest empty-pile move\u2014while preserving structured stack transfers\u2014can reduce churn without breaking the game\u2019s core progression.\n55. When losses are long, high-usage, and inconclusive rather than quickly exhausted, the game often has enough mobility but too many reversible parking routes. Tightening the broadest non-progress receiver is usually a better next step than adding more freedom, especially when that receiver does not itself count toward victory.\n56. When a game shows plenty of movement but zero wins, first ask whether its irreversible sink matches the patterns the tableau naturally produces. A sink that only accepts rare perfect runs creates churn; replacing it with frequent incremental progress often improves solvability more than adding extra mobility.\n57. When a game has heavy churn but zero wins, check whether the sink and win condition are even compatible with the deck\u2019s real completion units before loosening movement. A foundation count or target size mismatch can make every deal impossible while still generating lots of distracting legal moves.\n58. When a game shows long, high-usage losses with no wins, suspect reversible tableau churn before you add more mobility. The highest-leverage fix is often to turn an existing one-way receiver into the true sink and size its capacity to the deck\u2019s real completion units.\n59. When a solitaire shows long, high-usage losses with no wins, the problem is often not lack of mobility but lack of meaningful irreversible progress. A small change that turns an underused receiving area into a frequent, incremental sink usually improves solvability more than adding still more tableau freedom.\n60. When a solitaire already wins often but some losses exhaust almost immediately, the problem is often opening contact rather than overall difficulty. A count-preserving redistribution into more, shallower active piles can improve initial move availability and workspace without making the rules materially looser.\n61. When a multi-deck or reduced-suit game shows lots of movement but zero wins, the main problem is often not tableau mobility but sink compatibility. Broadening foundation acceptance to match the deck\u2019s duplication structure can convert churn into real progress with a smaller, safer change than further loosening general movement.\n62. When a solitaire shows high search activity but zero wins, the main problem is often not lack of mobility but lack of an irreversible sink that matches the structures ordinary play can create. In repeated-suit decks especially, rewarding full runs or chunks that the tableau naturally assembles is often stronger than forcing standard ace-up foundations onto an incompatible layout.\n63. When a solitaire already assembles one pattern naturally in the tableau, its irreversible sink should usually reward that same pattern. Requiring a different completion structure often creates many provably unwinnable deals even if mobility seems adequate.\n64. When a solitaire has no way to remove or permanently absorb the whole deck, losses that exhaust quickly are usually structural, not difficulty-related. Before tuning mobility, make sure the receiving piles can legally and capacity-wise represent the deck\u2019s real completion units, and align victory with emptying the active area into that sink.\n65. When a solitaire repeatedly fails with both short proved losses and long churny searches, check whether the game has an irreversible sink that matches the structures players naturally build. Requiring rare perfect completions as the only way to remove cards often makes a layout both strategically noisy and fundamentally unwinnable; allowing incremental progress into the same sink can fix both issues at once.\n66. When a game uses multiple copies of a reduced suit set, foundation rules should usually track the deck\u2019s real completion units exactly. If the sink groups cards too broadly (for example by color instead of by suit/copy), some deals become impossible or produce lots of reversible motion that doesn\u2019t convert into durable progress.\n67. When a variant fails before simulation, fix deck accounting with the smallest count-preserving structural change that preserves pile roles. A runnable near-neighbor gives much better design feedback than a speculative rewrite layered on top of an invalid setup.\n68. When a solitaire already has some full wins but most losses end at the search cap, the problem is often excess reversibility rather than lack of raw mobility. Reducing stock recycling is a high-leverage way to cut churn while preserving the game\u2019s core build rules and sink structure.\n69. When a solitaire shows high search depth without wins, look for one-way destinations that do not align with victory: they often create false progress and massive churn. Giving each pile type a clear role\u2014workspace vs. irreversible sink\u2014and making the sink the main route for ordinary progress is often higher leverage than adding more mobility.\n70. When a solitaire already wins on every shuffle with full card and pile usage, the best next adjustment is often to narrow the most permissive reversible move rather than changing the sink or adding new components. In tableau games, unrestricted fills to empty piles are a particularly strong source of easy mobility; rank-gating empties can add planning without breaking the core completion path.\n71. When losses exhaust in 0\u20133 moves with many zero-move starts, look for a structural impossibility before tuning difficulty. A one-way receiving pile should usually be the true sink and the win should align with filling it, not require an exact end-state that the rules cannot actually reach.\n72. When a solitaire already has some healthy wins but many losses end at the search cap with high usage, the problem is often reversible churn rather than lack of access. Tightening the most permissive temporary storage rule can improve decision quality and search tractability without redesigning the whole game.\n73. When a solitaire already wins often but some deals fail in just a few exhausted moves, suspect opening contact before sink mismatch. A count-preserving redistribution into more even active piles can reduce dead starts without broadly loosening move rules or increasing reversible churn.\n74. When a solitaire already permits lots of rearrangement but rarely finishes, the problem is often that irreversible progress is too rare, not that mobility is too low. Converting a sink from \u201conly accepts perfect completed chunks\u201d into \u201caccepts ordinary incremental progress in the same pattern the tableau creates\u201d is often more effective than adding yet more movement freedom.\n75. When a game produces long, search-cap losses with high pile usage, the problem is often not lack of mobility but that reversible play is disconnected from victory. One-way receiving piles should either be true winning sinks or clearly temporary workspace; splitting those roles across similar piles creates churn and false progress.\n76. When a game shows high card usage and long, inconclusive losses, the problem is often not lack of moves but lack of meaningful irreversible progress. In duplicated or reduced-suit decks, broadening sink compatibility to match the deck\u2019s real structure can improve solvability more cleanly than adding more tableau freedom.\n77. When a design has a one-way receiving area that is the only realistic place for permanent progress, the win condition should usually treat that area as the destination of completion rather than requiring it to be emptied later. Before tuning difficulty, make sure every structural role in the layout is consistent with victory.\n78. When a fully exposed tableau game already wins on every shuffle, first tighten the most reversible single-card mobility rather than weakening the sink. Restricting how empty spaces can be filled often raises decision quality and difficulty without breaking the game's completion path.\n79. When a solitaire variant is winning on every shuffle with full usage, first tighten the most generous reversible move rather than weakening the sink. Restricting who can fill empty tableau spaces is a small rules change that often raises decision pressure without breaking the game\u2019s core completion path.\n80. When a solitaire already has plenty of mobility but most losses end at the search cap rather than by exhaustion, the problem is often not lack of moves but a sink that accepts progress too loosely. Tightening a one-way sink so it rewards structurally meaningful sequences can reduce churn and make progress more legible without broadening the tableau.\n81. When a pile is a one-way receiver with no practical release path, it should usually be aligned with victory rather than required to empty later. Impossible solitaire designs often come from a mismatch between where irreversible progress goes and what the win condition asks for.\n82. When a solitaire has one-way receiving piles, the win condition should usually point at those piles as the measure of progress rather than requiring some other area to empty. After fixing syntax, prefer the smallest role-preserving repair so playtest data can expose the next real design problem.\n83. When a solitaire already wins on every shuffle, a good first difficulty lever is to narrow the most permissive empty-pile rule rather than changing the sink. Empty spaces often create the largest source of reversible churn, so rank-gating them can increase decision quality without breaking the game\u2019s completion path.\n84. If a solitaire has a one-way receiving area that naturally accumulates progress, victory should usually align with that sink rather than require the player to empty everything back out later. Also, any auxiliary storage that can receive from multiple places should usually have a clear release path; otherwise it creates dead states and search noise instead of meaningful decisions.\n85. When a solitaire already wins often but has a few fully exhausted unwinnable deals, first try a small count-preserving layout rebalance before changing core move rules. Slightly evening pile depths can improve opening contact and reduce shuffle-dependent lockouts without collapsing the game into triviality.\n86. When a solitaire uses duplicated or reduced-suit decks, foundation count and target size must match the actual number of complete suit sequences present in the deck. An otherwise playable tableau can become literally unwinnable if victory asks the sink to absorb more complete runs than the deck contains.\n87. When a solitaire file does not compile, prefer the narrowest rules-preserving repair that restores evaluation before attempting balance changes. Invalid pinned-card syntax is especially worth removing or simplifying first, because a runnable near-neighbor reveals whether the surrounding structure is viable.\n88. When a solitaire already has a one-way receiving area, make sure victory treats that area as progress rather than something that must later be undone. A contradictory win clause can make an otherwise playable ruleset provably unwinnable, and fixing that is usually higher leverage than adding mobility.\n89. When a solitaire already has a valid irreversible sink, repeated fully-exhausted losses often point to a mobility bottleneck rather than a victory mismatch. In that situation, adding stack movement that mirrors the tableau\u2019s existing build rule is a strong first repair because it increases meaningful reorganization without changing the game\u2019s overall objective.\n90. When a solitaire already wins on every shuffle with high card usage, the best next difficulty lever is often the broadest reversible multi-card move, not the sink. Tightening stack-transfer validity to mirror the game\u2019s intended building pattern can preserve structure and decisions while reducing easy churn.\n91. When a solitaire shows high move counts and high pile/card usage but no wins, the problem is often not insufficient mobility but lack of an irreversible path that aligns with victory. A one-way receiving area should usually either be the true sink counted by the win condition or have a strong release role; otherwise it mostly creates search noise and false progress.\n92. When most losses are fully exhausted rather than search-capped, suspect a missing or mismatched completion path before adding more tableau freedom. A sink that accepts the same structures the tableau naturally builds is often a more effective fix than loosening movement rules.\n93. When a game has one-way receiving piles, victory should usually align with filling them rather than later emptying them. If wins are impossible across many shuffles, check whether irreversible progress is being sent to a pile that the win condition ignores or contradicts.\n94. When a solitaire shows high card/pile usage but no wins, first suspect that irreversible progress is accumulating in a place the win condition does not value, or that victory demands an overly specific final distribution. Align the win with the game\u2019s actual sink behavior before adding more mobility.\n95. When a game\u2019s tableau builds broad compatibility patterns (like same-color runs across repeated suits), completion usually works better if the sink accepts those same assembled patterns directly rather than demanding a different incremental pattern such as ace-up exact-suit foundations.\n\nIf play shows moderate activity but zero wins, look for a sink mismatch before adding more mobility: changing what counts as irreversible progress is often higher leverage than loosening movement rules.\n96. When a solitaire rules engine effectively allows only one move rule per source/destination pair, merge special cases into a single OR tree instead of expressing them as separate moves. Restoring a runnable near-neighbor is usually more informative than redesigning around a compile failure.\n97. When a solitaire shows high card/pile usage but no wins, the problem is often not lack of mobility but lack of a sink that matches the structures ordinary play can actually build. In duplicated-suit or multi-deck tableau games, rewarding completed runs directly is often more effective than forcing progress through narrow ace-up foundations.\n98. When a game has plenty of mobility but no wins, look first at whether irreversible progress matches the structures players are actually able to assemble. A completion sink that rewards the same pattern the tableau already builds (for example, extracting full runs) is often more effective than adding more movement freedom or decorative foundation piles.\n99. When a solitaire already has a viable sink and some wins, but losses mostly hit search limits with high card/pile usage, the problem is often reversible tableau churn rather than lack of mobility. A strong, low-risk tuning lever is to narrow how empty spaces are reused\u2014especially by forbidding whole-stack moves into empties while still allowing a single anchor card.\n100. When a game asks the tableau to empty, first verify that its irreversible sink can legally absorb the entire deck. In duplicated-rank or multi-copy decks, foundation count often has to scale with the number of complete sequences present; otherwise no amount of tableau mobility can create wins.\n\nProduce the next version of skill.md: rewrite it as a whole, integrating what's genuinely new or well-supported from the insights above into the existing document rather than appending to it. Keep the entire document to at most 20 bullet points total, organized under a small number of clear headers. End your response with the complete new skill.md in a single fenced code block tagged `skill`, as the last thing in your response."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "```skill\n# skill.md\n\n## Establish a valid, playable core\n- Treat compile failures, bad deck accounting, invalid references, impossible predicates, rule-slot conflicts, and vacuous or contradictory win clauses as first-order design failures. Restore a runnable game before tuning difficulty.\n- Prefer the smallest count-preserving, role-preserving repair. A runnable near-neighbor reveals more than a speculative redesign layered on top of an invalid file.\n- Re-check setup, move rules, sink rules, and win clauses together after every structural fix. Reduced-suit, repeated-suit, same-color, or multi-copy decks can make familiar-looking color/suit rules vacuous or impossible.\n- Keep move definitions compact and non-overlapping. If the engine effectively allows one rule per source/destination pair, merge exceptions into one condition tree instead of splitting them into competing moves.\n- Treat stock/deal piles according to their real engine role. A deal-only stock is feeding infrastructure unless rules explicitly support direct play from it, and deal sizes should divide cleanly across the receiving pattern they feed.\n\n## Align sinks, deck structure, and victory\n- If victory requires active piles to empty, the design needs a reachable, irreversible sink that can legally absorb the whole deck under ordinary play. Rearrangement alone is not a completion plan.\n- Align victory with where irreversible progress actually accumulates. A one-way receiving pile with no reliable release path should usually count as completed progress, not be something the player must later empty or a pile the win condition ignores.\n- Match the sink to the pattern ordinary play naturally assembles. Incremental sinks that reward the tableau\u2019s native structure create real completion; sinks that only accept rarer or unrelated \u201cperfect chunks\u201d mostly create churn.\n- Scale sink count, capacity, legality, and target sizes to the deck\u2019s real completion units. In reduced-suit, repeated-suit, duplicated, or multi-copy decks, standard ace-up exact-suit foundations often misfit unless they are adjusted to the actual number and type of complete sequences present.\n- Keep pile roles crisp. One-way destinations should be true sinks or have strong release paths; decorative foundations, extra receivers, or broad storage piles that do not clearly support victory create false progress and search noise.\n\n## Diagnose losses: deadness, churn, and leverage\n- Distinguish deadness from churn. Very short exhausted losses often signal a missing completion path, impossible sink/victory logic, or poor opening contact; long high-usage search-cap losses usually signal reversible motion disconnected from victory.\n- When movement is plentiful but wins are absent, fix sink mismatch before adding mobility. In many variants, changing what counts as irreversible progress is higher leverage than loosening tableau rules.\n- When a game already has a valid sink and some wins, but most losses are long search-cap failures, tighten the broadest reversible routes before adding freedom. Common churn sources are unrestricted empty-pile fills, generic helper-pile parking, broad tableau exceptions, stack transfers through storage, and unlimited redeals.\n- Prefer constrained workspace over generic storage. Rank-gated empties, single-card anchors, stack rules that mirror ordinary building, specialized helpers, and limited recycling preserve planning value better than unrestricted parking.\n- If a game is already winning too consistently, the safest difficulty lever is usually to narrow the most permissive reversible move rather than weaken the sink or add new structure.\n\n## Tune openings and pacing with small setup changes\n- When losses exhaust in 0\u20134 moves across many shuffles, first verify a real completion path. If the sink and win logic are sound, then treat the problem as opening contact.\n- If some deals already win but many others die immediately, prefer small count-preserving setup redistributions over broad rule loosening. More even tableau depths, more shallow active piles, or slightly better exposure often improve fairness and early decision density without flattening the game.\n- When a valid sink exists and many losses are fully exhausted rather than search-capped, a mobility bottleneck may be real. Add movement that mirrors the game\u2019s existing build pattern before introducing broader, more reversible freedom.\n```"}]